.TH "CSSMATHCLAMP.CSSMATHCLAMP" "3JS" "August 11, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSMathClamp.CSSMathClamp \- CSSMathClamp: CSSMathClamp() constructor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]CSSMathClamp()\f[B]\f[R] constructor creates a new
\f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R] object representing a CSS \f[CR]clamp()\f[R]
function.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
new CSSMathClamp(lower, value, upper)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]lower\f[R]
A number or \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] that represents the minimum
value.
.TP
\f[B]value\f[R]
A number or \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] that represents the preferred
value.
.TP
\f[B]upper\f[R]
A number or \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] that represents the maximum
value.
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]TypeError\f[R]
Thrown if the parameters have conflicting unit types.
For example, mixing a length value with a time value.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
The following code creates a \f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R] instance from three
lengths, then reads back its \f[CR]lower\f[R], \f[CR]value\f[R], and
\f[CR]upper\f[R] properties.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] clamp = \f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathClamp(CSS.px(10), CSS.percent(50), CSS.px(500));

console.log(clamp.constructor.name); \f[I]// \(dqCSSMathClamp\(dq\f[R]
console.log(clamp.lower); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 10, unit: \(dqpx\(dq}\f[R]
console.log(clamp.value); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 50, unit: \(dqpercent\(dq}\f[R]
console.log(clamp.upper); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 500, unit: \(dqpx\(dq}\f[R]
.EE
.SS Handling incompatible types
The constructor throws a \f[CR]TypeError\f[R] if the three arguments
don\(cqt resolve to a compatible type.
In the following code we mix a length with a time, and log the error.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]try\f[R] {
  \f[I]// Mixes a length (px) with a time (s): incompatible types\f[R]
  \f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathClamp(CSS.px(10), CSS.s(2), CSS.px(500));
} \f[B]catch\f[R] (e) {
  console.log(e \f[B]instanceof\f[R] TypeError); \f[I]// true\f[R]
  console.log(e.message);
}
.EE
